    <item>
      <title>Numerical inverse scattering for the Toda lattice</title>
      <link>https://example.com/publication/toda-nist/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 16 Jan 2017 14:29:44 -0400</pubDate>
      <guid>https://example.com/publication/toda-nist/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Time evolution of purely dispersive initial data is computed at $t=10000$ without time-stepping, at least by setting $t=10000$ in the Riemann-Hilbert problem and solving different Riemann-Hilbert problems as $n$ varies. Below are plots of $a_n$ (the off-diagonal of the bi-infinite Jacobi matrix) and $b_n$ (the diagonal of the bi-infinite Jacobi matrix).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://example.com/publication/toda-nist/TodaSolution-NoS-a-10000.png&#34;
    alt=&#34;$a_n(t=10000)$, $-7500\leq n\leq 7500$. There are no solitons.&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;$a_n(t=10000)$, $-7500\leq n\leq 7500$. There are no solitons.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;

&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://example.com/publication/toda-nist/TodaSolution-NoS-b-10000.png&#34;
    alt=&#34;$b_n(t=10000)$, $-7500\leq n\leq 7500$. There are no solitons.&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;$b_n(t=10000)$, $-7500\leq n\leq 7500$. There are no solitons.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is the space-evolution of (truncated) contour for the Riemann-Hilbert problem that&amp;rsquo;s solved to compute the time evolution of initial data that generates a soliton to $t=1000$. Here $n$ varies from $n=-1250$ to $n=0$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://example.com/publication/toda-nist/Toda-1000.gif&#34;
    alt=&#34;Numerical contours for the Riemann-Hilbert problem as $n$ varies for fixed $t$.&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Numerical contours for the Riemann-Hilbert problem as $n$ varies for fixed $t$.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;

</description>
